        "original_id": "62889235-4384-552b-afed-b63f1ef3d165",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Heckscher State Parkway, Great River, Islip Terrace, Long Island, New York, Heckscher State Park, Great South Bay, Fire Island, New York City\n- Systems: NY 27\n- Topics: road geography, local geography\n\nFacts:\n- The Heckscher State Parkway, formerly known as the Heckscher Spur, is an 8.24-mile parkway on Long Island in New York, United States.\n- At Islip Terrace, the Heckscher State Parkway turns south, crosses NY 27, and ends at the toll barrier for Heckscher State Park in Great River.\n- Great River is a suburban hamlet and census-designated place in the Town of Islip in Suffolk County, New York.\n- Great River is located on the South Shore of Long Island about 50 miles east of New York City and adjoins the Great South Bay.\n- Fire Island protects Great River and the Great South Bay from the Atlantic Ocean.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
